#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# parametric_function

#% text
# `parametric_function(params, expr, data)` is the result of `parametric_n_dsolve_value`: `f(v1, ..., vm)` integrates the equations with the parameters set to the numbers `v1, ..., vm` and gives `expr`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The third argument holds the equations in the solver's own form; it is not meant to be written by hand.

#% item
# A symbolic value of a parameter leaves the call as it is; `table` and `plot` give it numbers.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
p = parametric_n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == -k*y(t), y(0) == 1], y(1), [t, 0, 1], [k]); p(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.3678794422457897

#% cell
p(2.0)
#% output
#= 0.13533528475265347

#% cell
[p(1.0), exp(-1.0)]
#% output
#= [0.3678794422457897, 0.36787944117144233]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several parameter values:

#% cell
table(p(k), k, [0.5, 1.0, 2.0])
#% output
#= [0.6065306602676048, 0.3678794422457897, 0.13533528475265347]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# `y(1)` as a function of the decay rate:

#% cell
plot(p(k), [k, 0, 3])
#% output
#= image("parametric_function.files/parametric_function-5.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `parametric_n_dsolve_value`, `n_dsolve_value`, `find_root`
